<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victual Brothers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1392">1392</a>–<a href="/years/1401">1401</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Vitalienbrüder, Likedeelers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Victual Brothers were privateers, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, who first appeared in 1392 supplying besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> for Albert of Mecklenburg against Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I</a>. They then preyed on Hanseatic shipping in the Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, occupying Gotland until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> expelled them in 1398. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> fleets defeated them in 1400–1401 and executed their leaders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaus-stortebeker">Klaus Störtebeker</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victual-brothers.jpg" alt="Victual Brothers" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vitalienbrueder,_Wandmalerei_in_d,_Kirche_zu_Bunge_auf_Gotland,_gemalt_ca._1405.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Victual Brothers, in German Vitalienbrüder, were a loosely organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of privateers who later turned to piracy. The Dukes of Mecklenburg hired them in 1392 in the struggle of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-i-of-denmark">Margaret I of Denmark</a> against Albert of Mecklenburg, who had been King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> since 1364. Margaret had imprisoned Albert and his son Eric to subdue Sweden, and her forces were besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> runners who shipped provisions into the city took their name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> victualia, meaning provisions.</p><p>Their main naval enemy in 1392 was the Hanseatic town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, which supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. The rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> at first supported the brotherhood, since most of its towns did not want a victory for Denmark. The Brothers had safe harbours at Rostock, Ribnitz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Brothers soon turned to open piracy and coastal plunder. They sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1393 and took Malmö in 1394, occupied parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, and plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku">Turku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a> and other castles. In 1394 they occupied Gotland and made Visby their headquarters, and they also operated from the Turku archipelago, where the castle's chief, Knut Bosson, supported them. Maritime trade in the Baltic virtually collapsed and the herring industry suffered. Queen Margaret even sought to charter English ships from King Richard II against them.</p><p>From 1395 Margaret gained the upper hand, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. Albert pledged Gotland to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, and an army under the Grand Master Konrad von Jungingen drove the Brothers off the island in 1398. Those who remained at sea called themselves Likedeelers, meaning equal sharers, who shared with the poor coastal population. They worked from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlei">Schlei</a>, the mouth of the Ems and Friesland, and raided Brabant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Their best-known leader was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaus-stortebeker">Klaus Störtebeker</a>, who first appears in the record as a Victual Brother around 1394. His name in Low German means Down the beakerful, and he allegedly earned it by swallowing four litres of beer without taking the beaker from his mouth, though it may simply be a family name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a>. In 1401 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> warship Die Bunte Kuh, leading a small fleet under Simon of Utrecht, caught up with his forces near Heligoland. After a running battle of three days Störtebeker and his crew were overpowered and executed.</p><p>Raiding did not end with him. In 1429, about 28 years later, members of the Victual Brothers plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> and burned it to the ground, and until about 1440 trade in the North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Seas</a> remained in danger of attack by the Likedeelers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Victual Brothers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Middle Ages its ships fought pirates on the North Sea; in 1401 a Hamburg fleet captured leaders of the <b>Victual Brothers</b>, and Klaus Störtebeker and his men were executed in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The towns then fought the pirates known as the <b>Victual Brothers</b>, whose leader Klaus Störtebeker was executed in Hamburg around 1401.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Military</a></li></ul></section>
